{
 "id": "2024-tural-sense",
 "name": "Sense of Spaciousness in Interiors: 16-Room Rating",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: the 16 rendered interiors from Figure 1 of Tural & Tural (2024), \"Exploring sense of spaciousness in interior settings: Screen-based assessments with eye tracking, and virtual reality evaluations\", Frontiers in Psychology 15: 1473520 (CC BY 4.0).\n\nParticipants see the same 15 ft × 15 ft consultation room in four sets of four renders, each set varying one design element: view access (window covering 100%, 66%, 33% or 0% of the wall), view content (woods, water, desert, lawn), boundary materials (brick, concrete, natural/biophilic, wallpaper with resilient floor) and ceiling geometry (flat, vaulted, curved, angled). Each render stays on screen for at least 18 seconds, as in the paper's screen session, and participants answer \"Please rate the interior setting in terms of the degree of spaciousness\" on a 1–7 scale. Each record is keyed by room id; the page source holds the paper's screen and VR means (N = 35) for comparison. Analysis as in the paper: repeated-measures ANOVA within each set with pairwise comparisons.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) online screen session only; the paper also had an eye-tracking session and a VR session with 360° panoramas, which are not available; (2) renders are taken from the published figure (about 875 × 490 px) instead of the 2160 × 1440 originals; (3) the paper does not state the order, so sets run in the paper's order with rooms shuffled within each set; (4) participants press Next after rating instead of advancing automatically after 18 s; (5) the scale anchors \"not at all spacious\" / \"very spacious\" and the design-background question are added.",
